    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media, from far-right to left-liberal, uniformly stress that the Ceuta surge has revealed deep structural flaws in the EU's external border regime and that a coordinated European response, rather than isolated national actions, is now seen as indispensable. At the same time, outlets across the political spectrum report the same sharp decline in asylum applications in Germany, using this data point to argue that policy discussions must reconcile reduced inflows with Germany's ongoing humanitarian and integration commitments.",
      "controversial_points": "The debate pits national sovereignty against EU burden-sharing, with conservatives demanding stricter borders and liberals urging collective responsibility. Media frames migrants as security threats or humanitarian victims, revealing a clash between security-focused right and rights-focused left. Legal experts and populist outlets dispute asylum procedural reforms, exposing tension between rule-of-law adherence and political expediency. Regional authorities and federal government conflict over integration funding, highlighting divide between local capacity concerns and national solidarity promises."
